Const-receiver rule is own-class-only: an inherited non-const method reached through a derived const receiver is silent on both sides of the file boundary


## Found by lane-enum while measuring B116, 2026-08-31. Left out of scope deliberately.

## The one fleet site, measured not guessed

`tm-map-together/src/Socket.as:467`

    skins[i].WriteToNetworkBuffer(buf)

where `skins` is `const Editor::SetSkinSpec@[]@`. `WriteToNetworkBuffer` is
non-const on `NetworkSerializable`, **two levels up** the hierarchy.

A parent-walking gate fires here. B116's own-class-only gate does not, on either
side of the file boundary — this is not a cross-file gap, B116 closed that.

Exactly **one** site in 151 plugins, out of 827 unique call sites reached.

## Why it was left out, and why that was right

There are two live possibilities and they want different fixes:

1. It is a **real missed error**, and the rule should walk parents.
2. `const T@[]` **element constness** does not propagate the way we model it —
   in which case the receiver is not const at all and there is nothing to fire
   on. The outer handle being const says nothing on its own about the elements.

Nothing in the repo distinguishes these. **Probe the game first.** Writing
either fix before that is picking an answer by coin flip and then building tests
that ratify it.

## The test that will flip

`b116_cross_file_const_receiver_tests::inherited_nonconst_method_agrees_across_the_file_boundary`

It asserts the two halves are **EQUAL** rather than hardcoding zero, precisely so
that closing this gap trips it instead of sliding past. Do not delete it; change
what it pins, and say which of the two possibilities above the probe settled.

## Test axes

- Inheritance depth 1, 2 and 4. MLFeed ships 4-deep hierarchies and B116 already
  measured 2 sites there (inherited-const, so silent and correct).
- A const method inherited from a parent (must stay silent) against a non-const
  one (must fire) — without the const half, a fix that fires on all inherited
  methods passes.
- The receiver spellings B116 already pairs: `const T&in`, `const T@ &in`,
  `const T@`, `const T`, plus `const T@[]@` and `const T[]` for the element
  question specifically.
- Same-file and cross-file for each, since B116 established the two arms are
  separate code paths and a fix to one is not a fix to the other.